<Layout title="FindHandle · BCQStudios" description="A case study on why matching handles across social platforms are worth holding.">
	<h1 class="mt-6 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">FindHandle.</h1>
	<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
		A case study on matching handles. One name people can type on every platform they already use.
	</p>
	<h2 class="mt-12 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Reach</h2>
	<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
		DataReportal's Digital 2025 series counts 5.24 billion social media user identities. In that same
		research, GWI finds the typical user visits 6.83 platforms a month, and half of adult users go
		there to learn about brands. On Instagram, 62.3% of active adult users say they research brands
		there. A name that holds on one network and breaks on the next is a name they cannot carry.
	</p>
	<h2 class="mt-12 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Habit</h2>
	<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
		People look for the name they already know. A 2017 WWW Companion paper found 45% of users reuse
		the same display name across networks. FairWinds, reading Interbrand's Top 100 in 2013, found 83
		of those brands owned their own Twitter handle. The habit is already there. The cost is leaving
		the name open.
	</p>
	<h2 class="mt-12 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Signal</h2>
	<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
		Lucidpress and Demand Metric asked organisations what consistent presentation is worth. In 2016,
		those with consistency issues estimated a 23% revenue lift if they always showed the brand the
		same way. 71% said inconsistency creates confusion in the market. Organisations with consistent
		presentation were three to four times more likely to report excellent brand visibility. In 2019
		the estimated lift was 33%. Those figures are survey estimates from a few hundred organisations,
		not proven causality.
	</p>
	<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
		A 2023 Journal of Retailing and Consumer Services study of 510 respondents found that when
		influencers keep information and image consistent across networks, followers identify more
		strongly, and purchase and word-of-mouth intention rise with that identification.
	</p>
	<h2 class="mt-12 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Trust</h2>
	<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
		A handle that does not match is easier to fake. In 2024 the FTC's Consumer Sentinel recorded
		845,806 imposter-scam reports and $2.95 billion lost to imposters, inside $12.5 billion in
		reported fraud losses. People who said a scam started on social media reported $1.9 billion in
		losses, and 70% of those reports included a loss.
	</p>
	<h2 class="mt-12 text-sm text-muted-foreground">The check</h2>
	<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
		FindHandle is a studio release. Type one handle. It looks at YouTube, TikTok, X, Facebook, and
		Instagram, then the .com, .net, .org, .io, and .ai domains.
	</p>
